statement:
One table records how long visitors stayed on an app:

- `Sessions(session_id, duration)`: one row per session; `session_id` is unique and `duration` is the session's length in **seconds** (a non-negative integer).

Build the data for a bar chart of session lengths in minutes using four bins, labelled exactly:

- `[0-5>`: shorter than 5 minutes (under 300 seconds);
- `[5-10>`: at least 5 and under 10 minutes;
- `[10-15>`: at least 10 and under 15 minutes;
- `15 or more`: 15 minutes (900 seconds) or longer.

Return the columns `bin` and `total`, where `total` is the number of sessions in the bin. All four bins appear in the result, with `total = 0` for an empty bin. Rows may come in any order.

validator:
function validate(input) {
  const [{ Sessions = [] }] = input;
  const seen = new Set();
  for (const [id, duration] of Sessions) {
    if (id === null) return 'Every session needs a session_id.';
    if (seen.has(id)) return `session_id ${id} appears twice in Sessions.`;
    seen.add(id);
    if (duration === null) return 'duration is never null.';
    if (duration < 0) return `Session ${id} has a negative duration.`;
  }
  return null;
}

generator:
function generate(rand, size) {
  const small = size === 'small';
  const pool = [0, 30, 199, 299, 300, 301, 580, 599, 600, 601, 899, 900, 1000, 5000];
  const count = small ? Math.floor(rand() * 9) : 2000;
  const ids = [];
  for (let i = 1; i <= count; i++) ids.push(i);
  // Shuffle so session ids arrive in no particular order.
  for (let i = ids.length - 1; i > 0; i--) { const j = Math.floor(rand() * (i + 1)); [ids[i], ids[j]] = [ids[j], ids[i]]; }
  const Sessions = ids.map((id) => [id, small ? pool[Math.floor(rand() * pool.length)] : Math.floor(rand() * 1500)]);
  return [{ Sessions }];
}
